# UI design language & concepts

Design direction: **editorial / calm**. Quappe is a discourse platform — text
is the product, so the UI treats text as a designed element (large serif
headlines, generous whitespace, a centred reading column) rather than something
to hide. Controls stay out of the way until needed. Mobile-first: one structure
for all widths.

Principles:
- **Editorial:** serif headlines (`--font-serif`, system stack — no web font),
  uppercase eyebrow meta lines, big type contrast, lots of margin.
- **Progressive disclosure / calm:** show the minimum needed to decide; details
  on demand. Chrome (budget, slider, menu) is hidden in popovers until invoked.
- **Two axes of reader control:** amount (complexity slider, Fibonacci) and
  density (author-provided reading registers: simple / prose / dense).
- **Direction-neutral, humane:** support == reject for visibility; a decaying
  germination boost lets good new positions surface. See `.project`.


# Tech stack

- SvelteKit 2 + TypeScript, Svelte 5 runes ($state/$derived/$effect/$props).
- No CSS framework, no UI component library. Hand-written CSS with variables
  (`src/app.css`).
- i18n via Paraglide (en base; de/fr/es). URL-prefix strategy.
- The UI is presentation-only: it talks to the server through page `load`
  functions and `/api/*` only — never imports `data.ts` or server modules.
  (Keeps `quappe-web` split-ready — see `.project` platform north star.)


# Shell — top-down (no sidebar)

Sticky top bar over a centred content column. The old left/right sidebars are
gone (that was the "dashboard" feel we moved away from).

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Quappe   Trending  Top  Mine  Updates•     [+ New] ▚ ◷5 ◐ ⋯  │  row 0 (sticky, frosted)
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                │
│            ╔══════════════════════════════════════╗           │
│            ║  Where positions take shape          ║           │  masthead (serif, landing only)
│            ║  Propose a thesis, weigh arguments…   ║           │
│            ╚══════════════════════════════════════╝           │
│                                                                │
│            ⌕ Search theses…                                    │  search
│            categories · #hashtags (filter)                     │  filter row
│                                                                │
│            ┌───────────────┐  ┌───────────────┐                │  centred grid
│            │ editorial card│  │ editorial card│                │  (max-width ~1000px)
│            └───────────────┘  └───────────────┘                │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Row 0 anatomy (left → right):
- **Brand** (Logo + "Quappe"). Name hides on mobile.
- **Primary nav** (centre-left): Trending · Top · Mine · Updates (with unread
  badge, pulsing). Horizontally scrollable on mobile.
- **Actions** (right):
  - `[+ New]` primary button (label collapses to icon on mobile).
  - Complexity slider — icon button `▚`, opens a popover with the slider.
  - Budget — pill `◷N` showing the tightest remaining bucket; opens a popover
    with all four buckets (theses / support args / reject args / weight pool)
    + recent activity + link to `/my#budget`.
  - Theme quick-toggle `◐` (invert on/off).
  - Overflow `⋯` — popover menu: Pulse · About · Settings.

Popovers are transient: close on outside click (a full-screen invisible
backdrop), Escape, or navigation. On mobile they become bottom-sheets.


# Views

## Landing (/) — "Trending"
Editorial masthead (serif title + subtitle) · search · category tiles
(size-scaled drill-down) + hashtag chips · centred grid of ThesisCards.
Complexity slider caps how many show. Live "already exists?" hints while
creating (links open in a new tab so the half-typed form survives).

## Top (/top)
Like landing, ordered by quality + support (+ germination boost); includes
`contested` theses. Rank badges.

## My (/my) and Updates (/my/updates)
`/my`: theses you created or voted on. `/my/updates`: a chronological feed plus
a fork-decision carousel at the top (one vote per fork family).

## Thesis detail (/thesis/[id])
Serif title (canonical) + translate button beside it · description in the
slider-bound register with a "Reading: simple/dense" indicator when a variant
is shown · categories · vote row · two argument columns (Supporting | Rejecting).
Each argument is a fork FAMILY rendered as ONE card: leading variant shown,
expandable radio-list picker (sorted Top/Latest, capped by slider, "+N more").
One vote per group.

## About (/about)
Own left mini-nav (sections) + reading body. No decorative image.

## Settings (/settings)
Anonymous ID · role badge · language · complexity bounds · categories mgmt.
(Theme/invert live in the top bar now.)


# Key components

- `Logo.svelte` — `/quappe.png`, sized.
- `ThesisCard.svelte` — editorial: uppercase eyebrow (category · lifecycle state
  · argument count), serif headline, muted body (register-aware, slider-bound),
  two slim left bands (heat outer, lifecycle inner, 5px), footer vote row.
- `ArgumentCard.svelte` — represents a fork family. Content, evidence badge +
  source chips (derived from URLs; users never pick evidence type), vote row +
  fork/edit icons, expandable variant picker. One vote per group.
- `VoteRow.svelte` — support/reject/neutral. Weight cycles the Fibonacci ladder
  1→2→3→5→8→reset. Base weight-1 is free; extra weight draws the daily pool.
- `ComplexitySlider.svelte` — discrete Fibonacci steps (theses 3·5·8·13·21·34·55,
  args 1·2·3·5·8, related 3·5·8·13), one index drives all three.
- `ActivityGraph.svelte` — pastel streamgraph, 5-day rolling average.


# Design tokens (src/app.css)

- Backgrounds: `--color-bg` #fafafa, `--color-surface` #fff, soft border #eaeef3.
- Accent: primary indigo #4f46e5 (theme-swappable: rainbow/pastel/grayscale).
- Vote colors: support green #16a34a, reject red #dc2626, neutral gray.
- Fonts: `--font-sans` (system), `--font-serif` (system serif stack, for
  headlines), `--font-mono` (numbers/IDs).
- Rounded, soft edges: radii sm .4 / md .7 / lg 1 / xl 1.4 rem; diffuse
  low-contrast shadows.
- `data-invert="true"` on <html> flips everything (independent of theme).


# Themes & accessibility modes

Two ORTHOGONAL axes, both applied as attributes on <html>, both stored in
localStorage, both surfaced in the header theme popover:

1. **Aesthetic theme** (`data-theme`, store: `theme.svelte.ts`) — one of
   rainbow (default, no attribute) · pastel · classic · unicorn · grayscale.
   Pure palette swap via CSS variables.
2. **Accessibility modes** (store: `a11y.svelte.ts`, plus the separate
   `invert.svelte.ts`) — independent booleans that COMBINE with any theme:
   - `data-invert` — invert all colors (dark-ish; re-inverts raster media).
   - `data-calm` — reduced visual noise: desaturate the surface (`saturate .7`),
     flatten shadows, remove card hover-lift, dim the decorative heat/lifecycle
     side bands, stop the badge pulse.
   - `data-contrast` — stronger text/border contrast, thicker card borders.
   - `data-reduced-motion` — neutralise transitions/animations + instant scroll.

## Naming decision (important — do not "simplify" back)
Accessibility modes are named by FUNCTION, never by diagnosis or politics.
The user asked for "ADHS / neurodivergent / conservative / unicorn" themes.
We deliberately split that:
- Aesthetic wishes → themes (unicorn kept; "conservative" became **classic**,
  i.e. muted/editorial — a political label on a neutral-discourse platform sends
  the wrong signal).
- Access needs → functional modes (**calm** instead of "ADHS/neurodivergent").
  Naming a mode after a diagnosis reduces people to that diagnosis, like calling
  a ramp a "disabled mode". "Calm" helps anyone distracted by a busy UI (ADHD,
  autism, migraine, or a noisy room) without labelling them. This is inclusive,
  fits the humanist mission, and is the reason the modes exist.

## Reduce-motion honesty
Quappe has little real motion — the only conspicuous, repeating animation is the
pulsing unread badge; the rest are subtle hover/popover transitions. So:
- We respect the OS `prefers-reduced-motion` media query automatically (users
  who set it system-wide need not find the toggle).
- The manual toggle is an additive convenience. If richer motion is added later
  (e.g. streamgraph transitions), both paths already cover it.


# Interaction patterns

- Voting toggles: same type+weight twice = retract. Weight cycles the Fibonacci
  ladder; base vote is free, extra weight costs from the daily pool.
- ThesisCard: whole card links to detail; vote buttons stop propagation.
- Fork keeps the source's stance; forking migrates the author's vote to the new
  variant; the family shows as one card with a variant picker.
- Reading register follows the complexity slider (author variants, with
  fallback to the original); a small indicator makes it legible.
- Editing costs no budget; creating and forking do.
- Anonymous identity is a server-minted JWT (httpOnly cookie).


# Later (UI)

- Simplified-language / TL;DR surfacing at low complexity.
- Command palette (⌘K) as an ADDITIVE power-user path (not the only way — the
  audience is broad, not developers).
- PWA / installable wrapper (no native apps; the API allows 3rd-party clients).
